  "question": {
    "text": "A vertical closed cylinder is separated into two parts by a frictionless piston of mass <math><mi>m</mi></math> and of negligible thickness. The piston is free to move along the length of the cylinder. The length of the cylinder above piston is <math><msub><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> and that below the piston is <math><msub><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> such that <math><msub><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math> Each part of the cylinder contains <math><mi>n</mi></math> moles of an ideal gas at equal temperature <math><mi>T</mi><mo>.</mo></math> If the piston is stationary, its mass&nbsp;<math><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;will be given by:<br /><br>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is universal gas constant and <math><mi>g</mi></math> is the acceleration due to gravity)",
    "image": null
  },
